From 9be2d2803cd667a813b86fd43fe112b13f3dc9d5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Marek Czernohous <marek@czernohous.de>
Date: Sun, 16 Aug 2026 09:49:53 +0200
Subject: [PATCH v2] drm/nouveau: drain the fence uevent work after tearing the
 event down

nouveau_fence_context_del() drains the uevent work before the event that
feeds it is gone:

	cancel_work_sync(&fctx->uevent_work);
	nouveau_fence_context_kill(fctx, 0);
	nvif_event_dtor(&fctx->event);

Between the drain and the dtor the event is still armed, and
nouveau_fence_wait_uevent_handler() queues the work unconditionally:

	schedule_work(&fctx->uevent_work);
	return NVIF_EVENT_KEEP;

A non-stall interrupt in that window re-arms the work that was just
drained.  The callers free the context immediately afterwards, for
example nv84_fence_context_del():

	nouveau_fence_context_del(&fctx->base);
	chan->fence = NULL;
	nouveau_fence_context_free(&fctx->base);

nouveau_fence_uevent_work() then runs against freed memory, taking
fctx->lock and walking fctx->pending.

Only chips from G84 on can reach this: nouveau_fence_context_new()
returns before nvif_event_ctor() when priv->uevent is clear, and
nv84_fence_create() is the only place that sets it.
nv84_fence_context_del() is the context_del for all of those, because
nvc0_fence_create() and gv100_fence_create() build on
nv84_fence_create() and override only context_new.

Move the drain to the end instead, leaving the kill and the dtor in
their original order:

	nouveau_fence_context_kill(fctx, 0);
	nvif_event_dtor(&fctx->event);
	cancel_work_sync(&fctx->uevent_work);

Each step then closes the door behind it.

The kill signals every fence on fctx->pending under fctx->lock and
removes it from the list.  After that dma_fence_add_callback() returns
-ENOENT for those fences before it reaches __dma_fence_enable_signaling()
(drivers/dma-buf/dma-fence.c:707-710), so nouveau_fence_enable_signaling()
and the nvif_event_allow() in it are no longer reachable.  This is what
makes the following dtor safe, and it is why the kill has to stay in
front of it: nvif_event_constructed() is a plain read of object->client,
not a lock, and nouveau_fence_context_del() holds nothing that would
serialise it against a concurrent enable_signaling().

The dtor then stops the handler for good.  nvkm_event_ntfy() delivers
under read_lock_irqsave(&event->list_lock) while it calls ntfy->func(),
and nvkm_event_ntfy_remove() takes write_lock_irq() on the same lock
before unlinking (drivers/gpu/drm/nouveau/nvkm/core/event.c), so the
teardown waits out any handler already running and no further one can
start.

The drain last therefore picks up whatever the handler queued on its way
out, with nothing left that could re-arm it.  nouveau_fence_uevent_work()
itself only takes fctx->lock and looks at fctx->pending, which the kill
has already emptied, so running it once more is harmless.

Found by the Sashiko review bot, which also caught the first attempt at
this: v1 moved the dtor to the front instead, which put it exactly where
enable_signaling() is still live and traded the re-arm window for a wider
NULL-deref window in nvif_object_ioctl().  That version is withdrawn.

Reported-by: sashiko-bot <sashiko-bot@kernel.org>
Link: https://lore.kernel.org/nouveau/178682366002.3748010.12779628082366287968@gmail.com/
Fixes: 39126abc5e20 ("nouveau: offload fence uevents work to workqueue")
Cc: stable@vger.kernel.org
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5
Signed-off-by: Marek Czernohous <marek@czernohous.de>
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/nouveau/nouveau_fence.c | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/nouveau/nouveau_fence.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/nouveau/nouveau_fence.c
index edbe9e08ba0f..f9e77436e4e7 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/nouveau/nouveau_fence.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/nouveau/nouveau_fence.c
@@ -96,9 +96,9 @@ nouveau_fence_context_kill(struct nouveau_fence_chan *fctx, int error)
 void
 nouveau_fence_context_del(struct nouveau_fence_chan *fctx)
 {
-	cancel_work_sync(&fctx->uevent_work);
 	nouveau_fence_context_kill(fctx, 0);
 	nvif_event_dtor(&fctx->event);
+	cancel_work_sync(&fctx->uevent_work);
 	fctx->dead = 1;
 
 	/*

